          Reliability in a refrigerator comes down to fewer parts: fewer doors, no dispenser, an optional ice maker, a clear compressor warranty and a technician nearby. Brand matters, but layout matters more.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-makes-reliable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Makes a Refrigerator Reliable: Fewer Parts, Not a Badge
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shoppers ask which brand is most reliable, but the honest answer starts with what is inside the box. A refrigerator is a sealed cooling loop, a compressor, a couple of fans, a defrost system and a control board. Every feature you add on top of that, an ice maker, a door dispenser, a fourth door, a Wi-Fi module, adds another part that can fail. A plain refrigerator from an average brand usually outlives a feature-heavy refrigerator from a good one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Layout Matters More Than Logo</h3>

          <p>
            Top-freezer refrigerators have the simplest construction of any full-size style: one fresh food door, one freezer door, and cold air that falls naturally from the freezer into the fresh food section. Bottom-freezers add a drawer slide. Side-by-sides and French doors add more doors, more gaskets, more fans and, almost always, a through-the-door dispenser. Owners commonly report that the simpler the layout, the fewer service calls they see over the life of the appliance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ice Makers and Dispensers</h3>

          <p>
            If one feature explains most of the gap between a reliable refrigerator and an unreliable one, it is the ice and water system. Ice makers involve a water valve, a fill tube that can freeze, a motor, a heater and sensors, and dispensers add a door chute, a flap, a heater and another water line. Repair technicians consistently name ice makers and water valves among the most common refrigerator service calls. Every pick in this guide either skips the ice maker or makes it optional.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Compressor Type</h3>

          <p>
            Most refrigerators use either a conventional single-speed compressor or an inverter compressor that varies its speed. Inverter designs run more smoothly and use less energy, and some makers back them with long parts warranties. A conventional compressor is a mature, well-understood part that is easy for any technician to diagnose. Neither is automatically more reliable; what matters is the warranty that comes with it and whether a local technician can fix it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brand-by-brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Most Reliable Refrigerator Brands, Brand by Brand
          </h2>

          <p>
            We are not going to rank brands with invented failure percentages. Reliability surveys exist, but they change year to year and they vary by style within a single brand. What we can tell you is how each major brand's lineup is built, what its warranty covers and how easy it is to get serviced. For a broader look at every brand by kitchen type, see our guide to the <a href="/best-refrigerator-brands/">best refrigerator brands</a>; this page narrows the question to dependability.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">GE</h3>

          <p>
            GE Appliances sells everything from basic top-freezers to Monogram built-ins, and its simple models have a long reputation for running for years. GE's biggest reliability advantage is service: it has one of the largest technician networks in the country and parts are widely stocked. Its plain top-freezers are the safest choice in its lineup.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whirlpool, Maytag and Amana</h3>

          <p>
            Whirlpool Corporation owns Whirlpool, Maytag, Amana and KitchenAid, and many parts are shared across those brands. That shared parts pool is a real advantage when something breaks. Amana is the stripped-down entry brand, Whirlpool the mainstream one, and Maytag leans on longer marketed warranties on some models, so read the specific model's terms.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Frigidaire</h3>

          <p>
            Frigidaire is the value brand of Electrolux. Its basic top-freezers are some of the cheapest full-size refrigerators from a major maker, and they are popular with landlords for exactly that reason. Its more complex models get more mixed owner reports, so the simple models are the ones to buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">LG and Samsung</h3>

          <p>
            LG and Samsung lead in features, styling and capacity, and both sell many French door and four-door models with dispensers, which raises the number of parts that can fail. LG backs its inverter compressors with long parts warranties. Both brands have faced owner complaints about ice makers on some complex models, so if you buy either for reliability, buy a simpler layout.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bosch</h3>

          <p>
            Bosch focuses on counter-depth French doors with a premium build. Its refrigerators are well regarded for build quality and quiet running, but its service network is thinner than GE or Whirlpool outside major metro areas. Check for an authorized technician near you before buying.
          </p>

